WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
Film Courage: You have two movies coming out&nbsp;
that star Bruce Willis. How did this happen?

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.000
Matt Eskandari, Writer/Director/Producer:&nbsp;
Sometimes when people ask me you just did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.080
two movies with Bruce Willis starring and they&nbsp;
think it just happened overnight or just one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:22.160
those things that kind of hit but as a filmmaker&nbsp;
I’ve been grinding and doing it for more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.840
a decade. I went to film school awhile ago,&nbsp;
I’ve been telling stories, writing scripts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.520
making movies. So to some people it feels almost&nbsp;
like this overnight thing but no, I’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.000
at this for awhile and it’s taken&nbsp;
awhile to get to that point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.000
my work is established enough where people will&nbsp;
come to me like Oh we want you to direct this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.960
film or we’ve seen your work and we feel like&nbsp;
your style (your vision) would really fit into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.400
the content that we’re creating. That’s&nbsp;
how those films came about is I did several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.800
independent films that got a lot of buzz (a lot of&nbsp;
attention) and they attracted producers were like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.800
Oh hey we’re doing these other movies, are you&nbsp;
interested? We’ve love to hear your take on them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.160
and it’s a process. It’s like a job interview,&nbsp;
right? You go in there and read the script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.840
and come up with your vision for how&nbsp;
you think that film should be directed.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.920
Film Courage: Can we hear more about this pitch?

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.560
Matt: Yes. As a director when&nbsp;
you’re going to direct a script from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.160
another writer or a studio project&nbsp;
or a production company has,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:35.040
you have a certain freedom to kind of&nbsp;
read the script and come up with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:43.280
vision for it and that includes everything from&nbsp;
a lookbook to cast to how you think the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.880
is designed and looked and the visuals and&nbsp;
everything just every little detail that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.840
you can go in there with describing how you think&nbsp;
that film should be made and it’s everything from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.120
we’re going to use a lot of steadicam and we’re&nbsp;
going to use handheld, we’re going to use these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:07.120
kind of lenses, we are going to develop this sort&nbsp;
of arc with the characters and it’s going in there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:13.760
with a confident vision of how you think that film&nbsp;
should be translated from the script to the screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.880
and that’s really what you’re doing as a director&nbsp;
when you read someone else’s script. You’re taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:25.600
that and sort of deciphering how you think&nbsp;
the best version of that film should be made.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.240
Film Courage: How was it to have this production&nbsp;
company was it that worked with Bruce or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.320
knew…there was another company that knew the&nbsp;
company that worked with him…approach you and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.680
did you feel like you were validated like&nbsp;
Okay now this is a real filmmaking thing?

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.960
Matt: Yes, I definitely feel like it was being&nbsp;
able to getting approached to direct a script from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:55.440
another writer that has A-list talent like Bruce&nbsp;
Willis attached is definitely sort of a Oh…Whoah!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:03.360
This is finally a little validation of my work&nbsp;
as a filmmaker that people are trusting me with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:11.520
telling this story. So it definitely elevated&nbsp;
the level of okay the stakes are really high on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:19.040
this one. I really need to step it up and make&nbsp;
sure that I bring it 100 percent of my passion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.040
and all the knowledge and experience that I’ve&nbsp;
learned as a filmmaker (an independent filmmaker)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:29.520
for the last decade about okay how do you tell an&nbsp;
interesting story? How do you incorporate elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:38.080
of indie filmmaking which is kind of like that&nbsp;
fast-paced, quick, shooting style into a project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:43.840
where I have a little bit more money to play with&nbsp;
but knowing that it doesn’t mean that you can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:49.680
wild with it right? There’s still those parameters&nbsp;
of it’s got to come in under schedule, under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:56.080
budget and you want to be able to deliver the film&nbsp;
so that people are always asking for the next one.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.480
Film Courage: Sorry…did they sign&nbsp;
on for both movies or was it one?

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.240
Matt: No, you do one and then once you…and&nbsp;
that’s part of the experience right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.680
That’s one of the lessons that you learn&nbsp;
as a filmmaker throughout your career and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:17.200
I’ve said it time and time again is work&nbsp;
leads to more work. So you do a project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.040
you write a script for someone and then they&nbsp;
are like Oh hey we’re looking for a director to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:29.120
direct this other project that we have, we really&nbsp;
liked your script Matt and that film that you did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.080
would you like to read this script and give us&nbsp;
your take on the project. So that’s one of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:40.800
things that both as a filmmaker and everyone in&nbsp;
the industry when you work with a DP, he’ll make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.120
a film, he might meet with a producer who will&nbsp;
hire him on a film that he ends up directing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:53.440
or you work with an actor and the film might turn&nbsp;
out to be really bad but that actor was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:05:00.720
incredible in the film so you end up hiring him&nbsp;
again on a different film. That’s really so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:06.880
of this industry and so much of my career as a&nbsp;
filmmaker has been just one project leading to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.680
the next in terms of the relationships within&nbsp;
that project and the people you meet and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.080
friends you make and the collaborations&nbsp;
that happen leading to the next thing.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.880
Film Courage: What was the emotion like when you&nbsp;
found out that you got the…was it the first one?

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.680
Matt: Yes, definitely the emotion that I&nbsp;
felt as soon as I realized I was going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:32.400
directing this film with Bruce Willis was a lot&nbsp;
of excitement. I was just kind of like Whoah! I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:37.680
better step it up, you know what I mean? I can’t&nbsp;
mess this up. I’ve got to do the best I can so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:45.120
it was sort of like this nervousness mixed with&nbsp;
motivation, drive, more dedication. Like okay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.960
I’ve got to step it up and do what I can to tell&nbsp;
the best story possible and direct the best film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:06:01.600
possible within the constraints that I’ve been&nbsp;
given. For me I’ve learned to have more of a stoic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:09.840
reaction in my career to things and I think&nbsp;
that is what has enabled me to have a sustained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:17.200
career is when bad things happen you have to&nbsp;
be able to look at it from a stoic mentality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:24.000
of okay…that’s bad but we’re going to keep going&nbsp;
and then when something really amazing happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.240
like you’re going to get to do or you’ve got&nbsp;
this cool thing or whatever…great, I love that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:33.840
that’s good, let’s keep going, let’s keep moving.&nbsp;
So it’s having that balance where you can kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.280
not let it take you in either one direction or&nbsp;
another is one of those things that I’ve learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:45.680
earlier on just reading about stoic philosophy&nbsp;
and how stoicism helps people in their careers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:52.800
and it’s something that goes back to ancient Rome&nbsp;
and it’s just something that I’ve learned and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:58.800
on set as a director I try to have that sort of&nbsp;
stoic mentality on set because if you have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:04.880
confidence and that sort of don’t let the little&nbsp;
things that are going on on set sort of drive you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:11.040
crazy because they will. If you let either the&nbsp;
highs or the lows hit you then you will lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.960
that sort of control and you won’t be able to just&nbsp;
go into it confident, almost like a chess match.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:23.200
Just go in there with confidence knowing that&nbsp;
as things hit you, you can just keep going, take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:28.160
the hit or then something great happens and then&nbsp;
okay this is great, enjoy that little second of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.720
and then just keep going. Don’t let that thing&nbsp;
kind of…don’t ride that high to the point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:37.920
you forget what’s in the present, what’s&nbsp;
in the moment, what’s happening now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:57.840
That’s really been my best bit of advice is to&nbsp;
internalize that stoic philosophy in your career.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.068
Questions for the viewers:

00:08:01.068 --> 00:08:03.595
If you could work with one&nbsp;
movie star, who would it be?

